    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.cartesian_product - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.cartesian_product.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.cartesian_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the Cartesian product of this graph with another.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.cartesian_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the Cartesian product of this graph with another.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.cartesian_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute the Cartesian product of this graph with another.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute the Cartesian product of this graph with another.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L51570-L51673){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.cartesian_product(other)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Cartesian product](../assets/graphs/cartesian-product.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"G is a path A–B–C (accent edges) and H is a single edge 1–2 (teal). Their Cartesian product has a node for every pair, drawn as a grid with labels A1 to C2 for the pairs (A, 1) to (C, 2): 6 nodes and 7 edges, a 3 by 2 ladder. The four accent rungs along the rows copy G's edges, and the three teal edges down the columns copy H's edge.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe Cartesian product $G \\square H$ has a node for every pair `(g, h)` of a node of this\ngraph and a node of `other`. Two pairs are joined when they agree in one coordinate and\nare adjacent in the other: `(g, h)` and `(g', h')` are linked if `g = g'` and `h`-`h'` is\nan edge of `other`, or if `h = h'` and `g`-`g'` is an edge of this graph. Products build\nfamiliar structures from simple pieces: a path times a path is a grid, a cycle times a\ncycle is a torus, and repeated products of an edge give hypercubes. They also model\nsystems with independent components, such as the joint state space of two machines that\neach change state one at a time.\n\nBoth graphs must be directed or both undirected (edge directions are kept).\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[other]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The second factor. Neither graph is modified.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new graph with `n * m` nodes. The node for the i-th node of this graph and the j-th\n    node of `other` (both in ID order) gets ID `i * m + j + 1` and the label\n    `\"(g, h)\"` built from the two labels. Every node attribute becomes a tuple\n    `(value in this graph, value in other)`, with `None` for a missing value. Each\n    product edge comes from one factor edge, whose weight and attributes it keeps.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If one graph is directed and the other is not.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe product has $n m$ nodes and $n|E_H| + m|E_G|$ edges, and its adjacency matrix is the\nKronecker sum $A_G \\otimes I + I \\otimes A_H$, so its eigenvalues are all sums of an\neigenvalue of each factor. Distances add: $d((g, h), (g', h')) = d_G(g, g') +\nd_H(h, h')$. Parallel edges in a factor give parallel edges in the product; when both\nfactors are simple, so is the product. The result matches NetworkX's\n`cartesian_product` (with tuple nodes rendered as labels).\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~strong_product`) \n\n:   Combine the Cartesian and tensor products.\n\n[](`~tensor_product`) \n\n:   Join pairs that move in both graphs at once.\n\n[](`~add_grid_graph`) \n\n:   Add a grid graph.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nEach node of the product is a pair `(a, b)`, and two pairs are joined when they agree in one\nplace and are joined in the other. A path of four times a path of three is a 4 x 3 grid:\n\n::: {#14716158 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\np4 = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(4)\np3 = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(3)\n\ngrid = p4.cartesian_product(p3)\ngrid.count_nodes(), grid.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(12, 17)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe labels give each node's place on the grid:\n\n::: {#e356c251 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncells = grid.get_node_df()\npos = {\n    n: tuple(int(v) for v in label.strip(\"()\").split(\", \"))\n    for n, label in zip(cells[\"id\"], cells[\"label\"])\n}\n\ngrid.set_node_attrs(\"pos\", pos).plot(layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"label\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"500\" viewBox=\"5.6 14.0 688.8 492.0\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"5.6\" y=\"14.0\" width=\"688.8\" height=\"492.0\" fill=\"var(--ect-3239c383)\"/>\n<line x1=\"619.0\" y1=\"250.0\" x2=\"461.0\" y2=\"250.0\" stroke=\"var(--ect-e5dfd507)\" stroke-width=\"2.2800000000000002\" stroke-opacity=\"0.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n<line x1=\"619.0\" y1=\"250.0\" x2=\"461.0\" y2=\"250.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"transparent\" stroke-width=\"8.0\" pointer-events=\